data.content:
Your project targets **.NET 10** and `ReportGenerator.cs` uses `File.WriteAllText`, `Directory.Exists`, and `Directory.CreateDirectory`. The best approach is the established **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — no need to write a custom wrapper.

## Recommendation: Adopt `System.IO.Abstractions`

### 1. Install the packages

```
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <TestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your class

Replace the static `File.*` / `Directory.*` calls with the injected abstraction:

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REPORT_OUTPUT_DIR") ?? "/tmp/reports";

        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}_{timestamp}.txt");
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

The key change: `File.WriteAllText(...)` → `fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(...)`, `Directory.Exists(...)` → `fileSystem.Directory.Exists(...)`, etc. Same API surface, just accessed through the injected `IFileSystem`.

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
mockFs.AddDirectory("/tmp/reports");

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(path));
var content = mockFs.File.ReadAllText(path);
Assert.Contains("Report: sales", content);
```

`MockFileSystem` gives you a fully in-memory file system — no temp files, no cleanup, fast parallel tests.

---

> **Bonus:** Your `ReportGenerator` also has `DateTime.UtcNow`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.WriteLine` statics. On .NET 10, `DateTime.UtcNow` should use the built-in **`TimeProvider`**, and `Environment.*` / `Console.*` would need small custom wrappers. Want me to generate those too?
